{"timestamp":"2026-07-23T08:50:24.730Z","sessionID":"ses_071d87c04ffe69WbJ1AYQ8qFbp","type":"message.part","role":"assistant","partType":"text","messageID":"msg_f8e2b20b0001JfZHYIHkeEdaYP","agent":null,"modelID":"deepseek-v4-flash-free","providerID":"opencode","text":"```rust\n// 1. Entry point — every Rust program starts here\nfn main() {\n    // 2. Declare two variables; Rust infers they are integers (i32)\n    let a = 10;\n    let b = 20;\n\n    // 3. Add them. The `+` operator returns a new value, stored in `sum`\n    let sum = a + b;\n\n    // 4. Print the result. `{}` is a placeholder filled by `sum`\n    println!(\"The sum is: {}\", sum);\n}\n```\n\n**Line by line:**\n\n1. **`fn main() {`** — Every Rust program needs a `main` function. When you run the program, this is the first code that executes. `fn` declares a function, `main` is the name.\n\n2. **`let a = 10;`** — `let` creates a variable named `a` and gives it the value `10`. Same for `b = 20`. Rust automatically figures out the type (`i32`, a 32-bit integer) — you don't have to write it.\n\n3. **`let sum = a + b;`** — `a + b` adds the two values together. The result (30) is stored in a new variable called `sum`.\n\n4. **`println!(\"The sum is: {}\", sum);`** — `println!` prints text to the terminal. The `{}` acts like a blank — Rust replaces it with the value of `sum`.\n\n**To run this:**\n- Save it as `add.rs`\n- Compile: `rustc add.rs`\n- Run: `./add` (Linux/macOS) or `add.exe` (Windows)\n\nThe output will be: `The sum is: 30`"}
