{"timestamp":"2026-07-23T08:52:13.094Z","sessionID":"ses_071d87c04ffe69WbJ1AYQ8qFbp","type":"message.part","role":"assistant","partType":"text","messageID":"msg_f8e2cd6ba001YohZVR5h9bwT1Q","agent":null,"modelID":"deepseek-v4-flash-free","providerID":"opencode","text":""}
{"timestamp":"2026-07-23T08:52:15.505Z","sessionID":"ses_071d87c04ffe69WbJ1AYQ8qFbp","type":"message.part","role":"assistant","partType":"text","messageID":"msg_f8e2cd6ba001YohZVR5h9bwT1Q","agent":null,"modelID":"deepseek-v4-flash-free","providerID":"opencode","text":"Yes, absolutely. Rust is **statically typed** like Java — you just don't *have* to write the types when the compiler can infer them. But you can always add them explicitly:\n\n```rust\nfn main() {\n    let a: i32 = 10;  // i32 = 32-bit signed integer\n    let b: i32 = 20;\n\n    let sum: i32 = a + b;\n\n    println!(\"The sum is: {}\", sum);\n}\n```\n\nRust's primitive integer types mirror Java closely:\n\n| Java | Rust | Size |\n|------|------|------|\n| `int` | `i32` | 32-bit |\n| `long` | `i64` | 64-bit |\n| `short` | `i16` | 16-bit |\n| `byte` | `i8` | 8-bit |\n| —     | `u32` | unsigned 32-bit (no Java equivalent) |\n| `float` | `f32` | 32-bit float |\n| `double` | `f64` | 64-bit float |\n\nJava uses `int`, Rust uses `i32` — same thing, different name. The `i` prefix = **i**nteger (signed), `u` = **u**nsigned. The number is the bit size."}
